explain.narrative:
## Core concept
UNION combines the result rows of two or more SELECT statements and removes duplicate rows from the combined result. Each SELECT must return the same number of columns in compatible positions. Column names in the combined result are taken from the first SELECT.

## Syntax
```sql
SELECT column_list FROM source_a
UNION
SELECT column_list FROM source_b;
```

## SQLite sample database
The following examples use a small fictional dataset. Each worked example starts with a repeatable setup script so it can be run independently in the SQL Playground.

## Worked examples
### 1. Combine customer names without duplicates
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2025;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2026;
CREATE TABLE sales_2025 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE sales_2026 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO sales_2025 VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO sales_2026 VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Esha','Hyderabad');
SELECT customer_name FROM sales_2025 UNION SELECT customer_name FROM sales_2026 ORDER BY customer_name;
```
Expected result: Asha; Bilal; Chitra; Dev; Esha

UNION removes duplicate result rows, so Asha appears once.

### 2. Combine city lists without duplicates
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2025;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2026;
CREATE TABLE sales_2025 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE sales_2026 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO sales_2025 VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO sales_2026 VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Esha','Hyderabad');
SELECT city FROM sales_2025 UNION SELECT city FROM sales_2026 ORDER BY city;
```
Expected result: Hyderabad; Mumbai; Rajahmundry; Vijayawada

The result is a distinct set of cities from both periods.

### 3. Combine aligned customer and city columns
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2025;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2026;
CREATE TABLE sales_2025 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE sales_2026 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO sales_2025 VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO sales_2026 VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Esha','Hyderabad');
SELECT customer_name, city FROM sales_2025 UNION SELECT customer_name, city FROM sales_2026 ORDER BY customer_name;
```
Expected result: Asha | Rajahmundry; Bilal | Hyderabad; Chitra | Vijayawada; Dev | Mumbai; Esha | Hyderabad

Both SELECT statements return two compatible columns in the same order. The repeated Asha-city pair is removed.

### 4. Combine filtered rows from both sources
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2025;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2026;
CREATE TABLE sales_2025 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE sales_2026 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO sales_2025 VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO sales_2026 VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Esha','Hyderabad');
SELECT customer_name, city FROM sales_2025 WHERE city = 'Hyderabad' UNION SELECT customer_name, city FROM sales_2026 WHERE city = 'Hyderabad' ORDER BY customer_name;
```
Expected result: Bilal | Hyderabad; Esha | Hyderabad

Each SELECT can apply its own WHERE condition before the result sets are combined.

### 5. Use UNION to create a combined reporting set
```sql
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2025;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS sales_2026;
CREATE TABLE sales_2025 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
CREATE TABLE sales_2026 (sale_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO sales_2025 VALUES (1,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra','Vijayawada');
INSERT INTO sales_2026 VALUES (4,'Asha','Rajahmundry'),(5,'Dev','Mumbai'),(6,'Esha','Hyderabad');
SELECT '2025' AS sales_year, customer_name FROM sales_2025 UNION SELECT '2026' AS sales_year, customer_name FROM sales_2026 ORDER BY sales_year, customer_name;
```
Expected result: 2025 | Asha; 2025 | Bilal; 2025 | Chitra; 2026 | Asha; 2026 | Dev; 2026 | Esha

The year literal makes rows from different sources distinguishable, so the two Asha records are not duplicates.

## Query behaviour and dialect notes
The examples target SQLite and use portable core SQL syntax. Column positions and compatible data types must align across set-operation branches. Result order is not guaranteed without an outer ORDER BY.

## Common mistakes
- Returning different numbers of columns from the SELECT branches.
- Putting columns in different semantic positions.
- Assuming UNION and UNION ALL treat duplicates the same way.
- Expecting output order without ORDER BY.
- Using incompatible types across corresponding columns.

## Best practices
Choose UNION when duplicate elimination is required and UNION ALL when every source row must be preserved. Align selected columns deliberately, add a source label when provenance matters, and sort only in the final combined query.

